  "detail": null,
  "summary": "I reviewed all 9 patients on 'DOAC dose review - week of 10/19/2026' under RBC-PHARM-07. 8 doses changed and 1 was already correct. I changed each dose by modifying the existing order (the old order is stopped and linked to the new one), and wrote a progress note on each changed chart with the values, the rule result and the cited records. I did not change any other medication.\n\nApixaban:\n- pt-0001 Kowalczyk: 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. Two criteria met: age 84 and measured weight 57.6 kg; creatinine 1.18 mg/dL.\n- pt-0002 Vance: 2.5 → 5 mg twice daily. Only one criterion now (age 82). Weight is back to 64.9 kg (143 lb) and creatinine to 1.21 mg/dL; the hospital reduced the dose during kidney injury in February.\n- pt-0040 Ashworth: 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. Two criteria met: age 83 and an outside creatinine of 150 µmol/L (1.70 mg/dL, St. Brigid 10/15). Weight 68 kg.\n- pt-0041 Duquette: 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. No criteria met, but he is on itraconazole (started 10/6, 12-week course), which the policy counts as a qualifying inhibitor. The dose should be looked at again when the itraconazole course ends.\n- pt-0045 Lindahl: 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. No criteria met, but the St. Brigid infectious disease letter of 9/18 lists darunavir boosted with ritonavir. Ritonavir is not on his clinic medication list.\n\nRivaroxaban (creatinine clearance by Cockcroft-Gault, actual body weight):\n- pt-0003 Delgado: 20 → 15 mg daily. Clearance 44 mL/min: age 76, 90 kg, outside creatinine 160 µmol/L (1.81 mg/dL).\n- pt-0038 Moravec: 15 → 20 mg daily. Clearance 56 mL/min: age 79, 96 kg, creatinine 1.45 mg/dL. The May reduction was calculated with ideal body weight, which the policy doesn't allow.\n- pt-0039 Bauer: 20 → 15 mg daily. Clearance 44 mL/min: age 81, 59 kg, creatinine 1.10 mg/dL. I used the 59 kg weighed in clinic on 10/14, not the 168 lb he reported by phone on 10/21.\n\nNo change:\n- pt-0043 Pellegrino stays on rivaroxaban 20 mg daily. Clearance 59 mL/min: age 75, female, 71 kg, creatinine 0.92 mg/dL. Because nothing changed, I didn't write a note for her.\n\nNo patient on the list was already on 2.5 mg while also taking an inhibitor, so none needed a warning to the prescriber.",
